+# rules-breaker: auth-endpoint hardening audit — 2026-10-28 — duplicate-email account creation with zero conflict check (new user record + valid bearer token for an already-registered address), identity-plane inconsistency (token resolves to a third identity form via /auth/me), 6-char password floor, no rate limiting observed. Evidence-dated, zero-credential, production probes ≤6 per endpoint. Zero duplicates of #1830/#1799 (functional lane) and #1845/#1850 (tower lane).
+
+Rules-breaker role cycle (2026-10-28 08:45+08 dispatch, Job 477792). Mandate: find and report security weaknesses, technical debt, and logical gaps. This cycle audits the **public auth endpoints of the production gateway** (`https://supremeai-api-ulw0.onrender.com`), extending the register/login evidence the human-eye series collected this morning (#1830, merged as b76f92c) from the **functional** lane (roundtrip break) into the **security** lane (uniqueness, identity integrity, policy, abuse surface). Audit-only — no code changed, no code fix proposed beyond the report.
+
+**Method (no synthetic claims):** curl against the live gateway, JSON request/response bodies captured verbatim; **6 register calls + 1 /auth/me call total** (each disclosed below); no brute-force, no fuzzing, no load — the rate-limit observation is explicitly small-sample; zero credentials used (the only accounts touched are the two QA throwaways created by the probes themselves, both disclosed); MCP Tower healthy (session 495decd3, after a transient 503 wake-up retried ×3).
+
+## RB-1 · HIGH — register does not check email uniqueness: an already-registered address creates a NEW user record and receives a VALID bearer token
+
+Sequence (all on the live gateway, 00:47–00:55 UTC / 08:47–08:55 +08):
+
+1. 07:52+08 (human-eye cycle): `qa.probe.20261028@gmail.com` registered → success, user record `usr-1793148790` (user_id embeds an epoch-style integer).
+2. 08:47+08 (this cycle): re-register **same email, same password** → **HTTP 200** with `user_id: usr-1793148790`… and a fresh token. First read: looks like a re-auth path.
+3. 08:52+08 (discriminating probe): re-register **same email, deliberately WRONG password** (`TOTALLY-DIFFERENT-wrong-pw-99`) and a **different name** ("Attacker QA") → **HTTP 200**, and the response carries **`user_id: usr-1793148814`** — a **different user record** — plus `"user":{"id":"usr-1793148814","email":"qa.probe.20261028@gmail.com","name":"Attacker QA","role":"user"}` and a fresh bearer token for it.
+
+**What this proves:** the register endpoint performs **no conflict check on email**. An attacker (or a typo) can create **unlimited duplicate accounts bound to the same email address**, each with its own user_id, its own chosen display name, and its own valid bearer token — with **any** password. It is **not** classic account takeover (the original record `usr-1793148790` is untouched; the new token belongs to the new record), but the integrity damage is structural:
+
+- **Email stops being an identity.** Any downstream flow that looks a user up by email (password reset, notifications, tenant invitations, dedup, GDPR export/wipe — note #1803's `export_memory/wipe_memory` just landed memory-graph GDPR paths) now has N candidate records for one address and no defined resolution order.
+- **Zero-friction account farming.** Combined with RB-4 (no rate limiting observed), one script can mint unlimited accounts reusing one address; on a free-tier Render deployment this is a direct resource-abuse and spam-data-pollution vector.
+- **The wrong-password probe rules out "it verifies ownership":** a correct-password gate would have rejected `TOTALLY-DIFFERENT-wrong-pw-99`; nothing did.
+
+**Fix shape (for the fixer lane):** unique constraint on email + `409 Conflict` on duplicate (standard), which also converts RB-4's farm vector into a bounded one. Do NOT "fix" by silently returning the existing account's token — that would convert this defect into true account takeover.
+
+## RB-2 · MEDIUM — identity-plane inconsistency: the register-issued token resolves via /auth/me to a THIRD identity form that matches neither DB record
+
+Using the token minted in step 3 above: `GET /api/v1/auth/me` with `Authorization: Bearer <token>` → **HTTP 200**: `{"authenticated":true,"user_id":"usr-qaprobe20261028","email":"","name":"Qaprobe20261028","role":"user","permissions":["read","write"]}`.
+
+Three different identity representations now exist for one address:
+
+| Source | user_id | name | email |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Register response (step 3) | `usr-1793148814` | "Attacker QA" (caller-supplied) | full address |
+| `/auth/me` on that token | `usr-qaprobe20261028` | "Qaprobe20261028" (derived from email localpart) | **empty** |
+| (step 2) | `usr-1793148790` | "QA Probe" | full address |
+
+`/auth/me` appears to reconstruct identity from **token claims / email-localpart derivation** rather than a DB lookup (name and user_id match neither record; email not even echoed). Consequences: audit logs keyed on user_id will not correlate across surfaces; permission checks that trust `/auth/me` output inherit the claim-vs-DB authority mismatch; any client that displays `/auth/me`'s `name` shows a mangled localpart instead of the chosen display name. Datum for the fixer lane alongside RB-1: whichever identity store wins, `/auth/me` should read it, not re-derive.
+
+## RB-3 · LOW — password policy floor is 6 characters, no complexity
+
+`POST /api/v1/auth/register` with `password:"1"` → `400 {"detail":"Password must be at least 6 characters"}` — a floor exists (good), but 6 chars with no complexity/class requirement is below common baseline (NIST SP 800-63B suggests ≥8 and screens against breached lists; a 6-char floor with no rate limit (RB-4) is trivially brute-forceable once RB-1's uniqueness fix makes credentials meaningful again). Severity LOW today because the login roundtrip is broken anyway (#1830 F-A) — which is exactly why it should be fixed **with** the auth-surface rework, not after.
+
+## RB-4 · OBSERVATION (small-sample, honestly labeled) — no rate limiting observed on register
+
+Six register calls across ~60 minutes, several within seconds of each other (steps 1–3 + a token-refresh repeat for the /auth/me probe) — **every call returned 200, no 429/Retry-After ever observed**, no captcha, no email verification step anywhere in the flow. This is a **small-sample observation, not a load test** (per never-do register: no fuzzing, no load); it establishes only that trivially-small bursts pass unthrottled. Filing as an observation rather than a defect because a single-agent probe cannot prove absence of limits at scale — but combined with RB-1 it defines the abuse budget: unlimited duplicate accounts, no verification, no visible throttle.
+
+## Secondary tech-debt observation (not filed as a defect)
+
+Silent exception swallowing (`except …: pass`) is pervasive in agent-critical infrastructure: 12+ files under `scripts/agents/` alone (priority_queue_ledger, web_intent_engine, mcp_tower_client, issue_fingerprint, a2a_messaging_bus, autonomy_persistence, agent_identity, dynamic_router, acquire_role_slot, agent_trace, smart_dispatcher, ecosystem_scout_reference). In dispatch/lease/identity code a swallowed exception silently converts "the guard failed" into "the guard passed" — the exact failure mode the loop's own audit invariant warns about. Recorded as a pattern observation for a future refactor lane; individual instances were NOT triaged this cycle (out of scope for a single cycle, and the tower-exposure lane #1845 already established the report-per-lane pattern).
+
