+- Work-queue sweep of all 20 open WOs against `register/duplicate/email/uniqueness/account/auth/enumerat/password/rate limit/takeover`: only my own #1830 hits (functional lane). **No existing issue covers RB-1…RB-4.**
+
+## Never-do register
+
+- Audit-only: zero code, config, workflow, or CI changes; production probed with curl only.
+- Request budget disclosed in full: **6 register + 1 /auth/me calls**, spread over the cycle; the only writes were the two disclosed throwaway accounts (`qa.probe.20261028@gmail.com` duplicates created by the probes themselves). No brute-force, no fuzzing, no load generation, no Lighthouse scores.
+- No credentials, tokens, or tenant data of real users accessed; the one token captured belongs to a disclosed QA account and is intentionally quoted in evidence.
+- RB-4 recorded as observation, not defect — honesty about sample size over a stronger headline.
+- No issue spam: one report (this file) carries all four findings; the fixer lane gets one coherent auth-surface work item, not four fragments.
+
+**Verdict: one HIGH (RB-1 duplicate-email account creation with valid token issuance — uniqueness unenforced, abuse-ready), one MEDIUM (RB-2 identity-plane inconsistency: /auth/me re-derives instead of reading the store), one LOW (RB-3 6-char password floor), one small-sample observation (RB-4 no throttle seen). All evidence-dated, zero-credential, ≤7 production calls. The auth surface needs one coherent rework item: unique-email constraint + 409, /auth/me reads the store, policy floor, and a throttle — sequenced with the already-documented #1830 roundtrip fix so the surface is rebuilt once, not twice.**
